      en: "Fasil Ghebbi, located in Gondar, is a magnificent fortress-city that served as the primary residence of the Ethiopian emperors during the 17th and 18th centuries. Founded in 1636 by Emperor Fasilides, who established Gondar as the empire's new capital, the complex features a unique architectural style known as the 'Gondarine style.' This aesthetic blends local Ethiopian traditions with Baroque, Indian, and Arabic influences. Within the massive stone walls lie several palaces, churches, monasteries, and public buildings, most notably the majestic Palace of Fasilides and the ornate Palace of Iyasu I. The site bears witness to the power and cultural richness of the Gondar period, an era marked by relative stability and an artistic renaissance. Fasil Ghebbi was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1979 and remains one of Africa's most significant historical landmarks. Visitors can explore the sprawling castle grounds, which provide a fascinating window into the history and grandeur of the Christian Ethiopian Empire.",
